{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"S4 plan-review v3: BLOCK blocking=4 — and 3 of the 4 were FALSE findings caused by MY OWN misuse of the gate. review-plan defaults to --ref origin/main, i.e. an ATTESTED SNAPSHOT PREDATING my stack. S1 and S2 are landed in the STACK (changes 1012/1014, both +1/+1) but NOT SUBMITTED, so the reviewer was reading pre-S1/S2 main where _NATIVE_OUTPUT_PROVIDERS still exists and output_mode() still string-matches a provider prefix. Its findings 2/3/4 (profile override does not connect to output-mode dispatch) are CORRECT about main and WRONG about my branch — S2 deleted that frozenset and rewired output_mode to take a capability record. The tool documents this exact case: --ref REF says pass --ref HEAD when the review depends on code committed locally but not yet landed on the default ref (a stacked change), else symbols it adds read as does-not-exist FALSE FINDINGS. I was not using it. This likely inflated S2s six review passes too. Switching to --ref HEAD; NO early land needed (I had considered submitting S1+S2 early to fix the ref, which would have been the wrong tool for this). Finding 1 (E4) was a GENUINE correction: my plan asserted the openai SDK is not installed, which I made false myself by pip-installing it for the dev extra. Reworded to the accurate claim (not a RUNTIME dependency; agents extra omits it; dev only) and — more importantly — replaced the rationale so it no longer depends on SDK absence at all: with no base_url configured rebar has NOTHING to contribute to provider construction (no endpoint, no key, no profile override), so interposing a builder that merely reconstructs what pydantic-ai would build adds maintenance surface and divergence risk for zero benefit. rebar builds a provider only when it has configuration to apply — the same rule S1 established for Anthropic (it injects a retrying transport)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785378880449961001,"uuid":"9924f14f-e36e-44b2-abec-86b668f55eda"}